a. What is Strategy Pattern?
Strategy is a behavioral design pattern that defines a family of algorithms, encapsulates each one into a separate class, and makes them interchangeable — without changing the client code that uses them.

Core idea: Extract what varies, program to an interface, not an implementation.

Real-world analogy: Navigation App

Your destination stays the same (Context)
But you can choose: Drive / Bike / Walk (Strategy)
Switch anytime — the app still works perfectly

b. Why Strategy Pattern?
❌ Without Strategy — the problem
javapublic class PaymentService {
    public void pay(String type, double amount) {
        if (type.equals("credit")) {
            System.out.println("Paying " + amount + " via Credit Card");
        } else if (type.equals("paypal")) {
            System.out.println("Paying " + amount + " via PayPal");
        } else if (type.equals("crypto")) {
            System.out.println("Paying " + amount + " via Crypto");
        }
        // Adding a new method → modify this class → violates Open/Closed Principle ❌
    }
}
ProblemDescriptionViolates Open/ClosedEvery new method requires modifying existing codeViolates Single ResponsibilityOne class handles all payment logicHard to testCan't unit test each payment type in isolationif-else explosionGets unmanageable as business grows
✅ What Strategy solves

Add new algorithm → add a new class, touch nothing else (Open/Closed Principle)
Algorithms can be swapped at runtime dynamically
Each strategy is independently testable
Eliminates if-else / switch chains



